{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfwxjFZ7nJqp7MpPMi8Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Answer: Dq ≈ 1037 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 822 cm⁻¹.** The fit leaves C undetermined.\n\n**Model.** For a high-spin octahedral d⁷ ion the ground term is ⁴T₁g(F). The three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g = 5Dq − 7.5B + ½R\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴A₂g = 15Dq − 7.5B + ½R\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₁g(P) = R\n\nHere R = √(225B² + 100Dq² + 180·Dq·B).\n\n**Which band is which.** ν₂ and ν₃ are only 770 cm⁻¹ apart, so I tested both orders.\n\n1. **ν₂ = 19 550 cm⁻¹ and ν₃ = 20 320 cm⁻¹.**\n   - ν₂ − ν₁ = 10Dq = 10 370 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 1037 cm⁻¹.\n   - From ν₁: −7.5B + ½R = 9180 − 5185 = 3995.\n   - Setting ½R = 10 160 (ν₃ = R = 20 320) gives B = 822 cm⁻¹.\n   - Check: with Dq = 1037 and B = 822, R = √(152.0 + 107.5 + 153.4)×10⁶ ≈ 20 320 cm⁻¹. This matches ν₃.\n2. **The reverse order** (ν₂ = 20 320, ν₃ = 19 550 cm⁻¹).\n   - This gives Dq = 1114 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 822 cm⁻¹.\n   - But those values give R ≈ 21 000 cm⁻¹, not 19 550 cm⁻¹, so this assignment does not hold together and is rejected.\n\n**Full fit.** I fitted all three bands to the complete d⁷ ligand-field calculation as a check. It gave Dq = 1036.9 cm⁻¹ and B = 821.9 cm⁻¹, with every band reproduced to within 0.6 cm⁻¹. The predicted bands are 9181, 19 550 and 20 320 cm⁻¹, assigned to ⁴T₂g, ⁴A₂g and ⁴T₁g(P).\n\n**About C.** The three quartet (spin-allowed) bands do not depend on the Racah parameter C, so C cannot be found from this spectrum. The fit simply used a default value of C = 4499 cm⁻¹.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1037, \"B\": 822, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"The 4T2g, 4A2g and 4T1g(P) bands fix Dq and B, but these quartet bands carry no information on C.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2494,"cache_read_input_tokens":7985,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m